{"kind":"request","text":"Reporting artifact: 5343a64b18dc8308a42e692e7bb9383886677005 (internal/app/association.go at a5c0889f, newest on promise a4c569b1). Codex: fresh independent Architecture, Security and Simplification review of stage S1, at exact head a5c0889fa3e08f048eb1b09057af26da62ad3882 on request/lane-worktree-identity (pushed). This head answers your fourth changes-requested verdict, e389d856 (full id git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e389d8562ce26bac1f65c07f251d7260d4e21fd4) on e962fa7b, ratified 8e5d50ee.\n\nWhat governs it. The implementation request is still ac54fe71 (#22310) under my promise a4c569b1, a stage child of #22168 governed by Hugh's effective adoption ba18cd1a of proposal 7a71886b. Four correction rounds have not moved the outcome, conditions, performer, destination or governing authority, so all of them stayed on that one commitment. Sibling stages 0e7c04c8 (#22311, S2) and 8415ce89 (#22312, S3) are neither delivered nor claimed, and parent #22168 stays open.\n\nWhat to read first. The reporting artifact is the internal/app/association.go artifact, published last among the artifacts resting on the promise. It remains the direct current-head account you asked for -- what S1 delivers path by path, the conditions met one by one, the bound and the completion behaviour stated once, the current tests, the twenty-seven controls, the eight gates, what S1 deliberately does not do, and one review-history paragraph, now four verdicts by full id with a line each. Nineteen companion artifacts stand at this same head, one per changed path; the seven whose path changed since e962fa7b -- internal/app/checkout_read.go, internal/app/checkout_read_test.go, internal/app/worktree_landing.go, internal/app/worktree_index_test.go, docs/reference/architecture.md, docs/reference/landing-observations.md and notes/2026-09-09-lane-identity-reconciled.md -- each cite this verdict and say what changed at that path.\n\nWHAT CHANGED SINCE e962fa7b: seven files, +186/-33, one change per finding.\n\nFinding 1, the disposition predicate. You reproduced it on that head with a positive control: a receipt-backed settled checkout, first eligible with a complete table, then 256 ordinary refs added at the same held head, and a fresh capture through AdviseCheckouts returning associations.complete=false with the tip-limit reason, read.Exhausted()==false, and deletable still naming the checkout with its Row and Rows populated. AdviseCheckouts now publishes only when the shared read is neither exhausted nor cancelled AND the association table is complete. Everything the association can stop on is covered: the shared budget and deadline, the tip limit, the per-tip depth limit, an unavailable captured tip, an unreadable or over-bound ref inventory, and cancellation inside the association. When it withholds, it withholds everything -- empty candidate list, every checkout unknown for classification and grade with Governing, Row, Rows, Approved, LandedInto and RemoteContains cleared, table incomplete -- and it carries the reason that names the bound: the table's own where it has one, the exhaustion reason otherwise. unknownWorktrees now takes that reason rather than hard-coding exhaustion, so the classification and the table agree about which bound stopped the read.\n\nTests: TestAnIncompleteAssociationWithholdsTheCleanupAdviceToo, with three subtests driving the tip limit (257 refs at the held head), the per-tip depth limit (a 513-commit chain on a separate branch) and an unavailable tip (a second checkout with an unheld forty-hex head). Each leaves the shared budget and deadline untouched and runs through AdviseCheckouts, the production pair-producing entry point, because an association-only test cannot establish a property of the response. Each starts from eligibleCheckout -- a settled commitment whose sealed receipt witnesses its exact tip -- and requirePublished asserts that checkout really is offered and graded before requireWithheld asserts it is not, so no subtest can pass by refusing everything. Mutants: m26-predicate-ignores-table, where the predicate looks only at the shared budget and deadline again, and m27-withheld-reason-generic, where every bound reports as exhaustion. Both killed.\n\nFinding 2, the overclaim. You are right that ClassifyCheckouts and AssociateCheckouts remain exported and that the tests call them separately, so \"the only way any caller can obtain both answers\" was false. I have not added an API restriction to make it true; I have replaced the claim with the narrower verified one in all four places it appeared -- the doc comment on AdviseCheckouts, architecture.md layer 7, landing-observations.md and the reconciled note: AdviseCheckouts is the one response-disposition step, and handleWorktrees obtains its pair only through it, while the two judgments stay separately callable for tests. If you would rather have the enforcement than the description, say so; that is a design choice I did not want to make inside a correction round.\n\nThe provenance point you asked to be stated explicitly is stated explicitly, in the report and here. The candidate's own internal/app/app.go predates the landed oversized-attachment signing changes of #22093, because this branch is based on 359b211c. The clean merge tree onto current main c0108be8 carries those landed changes forward. The absence of a describes_superseded_world flag on the app artifact is evidence about that artifact's bases, and it is not evidence that those signing changes are present in the candidate snapshot; the two are different claims and I am not conflating them. No loss in the merge tree is observed or asserted, and the older branch base is not being offered as a reason to rebase.\n\nEvidence attached: gates-s1-v6.txt and mutants-s1.txt.\n\nGates, eight, all rc=0 and all fresh at this head; nothing is carried. Each rc is the command's own exit status under set -o pipefail: gofmt -l ./internal ./cmd; go vet ./...; go build ./...; go test -count=1 over internal/app, internal/gitstore, internal/service, internal/reviewguard and internal/statusview; go test -race -count=1 ./internal/app/; go test -count=1 ./internal/docset/..., green on its first run; go test -count=1 ./...; git diff --check.\n\nMutants, twenty-seven, all run against this tree and all killed; none survived. The twenty-five earlier controls were re-run, with m23 retargeted to the corrected predicate; m26 and m27 are new.\n\nBases. Every artifact of this set rests only on predecessors that are live, at a commit that is an ancestor of main, at the artifact's own path, and not flagged describes_superseded_world. No checkpoint artifact and no artifact of any earlier head of this branch appears. I re-derived that from a fresh projection at depth 22706 and asserted it in code rather than carrying last round's list forward; the flagged checkpoint basis 640c397d at 59778345 is filtered out by the same rule that filtered it before.\n\nWhat I am asking you to check. Your earlier conclusions covered the grading, the corroboration rules, the cache inputs, the shared captured read, the authority separation and the cleanup protections. This round is the predicate and the claims:\n\n1. That the disposition predicate now matches the documented incomplete conditions exactly -- no bound that can leave the table incomplete is missing from it, and none of them can publish advice.\n2. That the reason carried into a withheld response really does name the bound, in both halves, and that m27 is a real control over that rather than over a string.\n3. That the three subtests drive their bounds without touching the shared budget or deadline, that their positive controls are genuinely eligible, and that they prove a property of the response rather than of the association.\n4. That withholding still only turns answers into unknown, reads no claim, and weakens no existing protection.\n5. That the corrected description of AdviseCheckouts is now true in all four places, and that no absolute claim survives anywhere in the request, the artifacts or the pages.\n6. That the bases are as stated, and that the provenance distinction is put the way you asked.\n7. That the head still assigns and claims no S2 or S3 outcome.\n\nOne thing I am telling you rather than letting you find it: ui/src/lib/api.ts is still deliberately not extended and the wireparity gate does not cover WorktreeView, so no gate catches drift there. You have not made that a blocker and I have not changed it.\n\nMerge base. This head is one commit over main 359b211c. Current main is c0108be8. git merge-tree --write-tree main a5c0889f is clean, producing tree 8226bc079a769174fe68cea740bdde0992ed8184. The twenty artifacts published at e962fa7b are live at these same twenty paths but sit outside the merge target on this branch, so they are not bases of this set and this merge does not retire them; I retire them separately naming these as successors.\n\nFile the verdict with gs review, naming the internal/app/association.go artifact first, so the lane resolves through the reporting artifact. I will ratify the report.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:5343a64b18dc8308a42e692e7bb9383886677005","conditions":"Fresh independent Architecture, Security and Simplification review of the exact head a5c0889fa3e08f048eb1b09057af26da62ad3882 on request/lane-worktree-identity, against implementation request ac54fe71 (#22310) under the unchanged promise a4c569b1 and the adopted design ba18cd1a/7a71886b, reading the twenty artifacts published at that head with the internal/app/association.go artifact as the reporting artifact. This head answers the fourth changes-requested verdict e389d856 on e962fa7b, ratified 8e5d50ee, in seven files, +186/-33. Confirm both findings are discharged. The disposition predicate: AdviseCheckouts publishes only when the shared read is neither exhausted nor cancelled and the association table is complete, so every bound that can leave the table incomplete -- the shared budget and deadline, the tip limit, the per-tip depth limit, an unavailable captured tip, an unreadable or over-bound ref inventory, and cancellation inside the association -- withholds the whole response, with an empty candidate list, every checkout unknown for classification and grade with Governing, Row, Rows, Approved, LandedInto and RemoteContains cleared, and both halves carrying the reason that names the bound rather than reporting every bound as exhaustion; check that no incomplete condition is missing from the predicate, that the three new subtests drive their bounds without touching the shared budget or deadline and start from genuinely eligible positive controls, and that withholding still only turns answers into unknown, reads no claim and weakens no existing protection. The overclaim: confirm that no absolute \"only way\" claim survives in the code comment, architecture.md, landing-observations.md, the reconciled note, this request or any artifact, and that what stands in their place -- AdviseCheckouts is the one response-disposition step and handleWorktrees obtains its pair only through it, with the two judgments separately callable for tests -- is true; say if you would rather have the API restriction enforced than described. Confirm the bases: every artifact rests only on predecessors that are live, at a commit that is an ancestor of main, at the artifact's own path, and not flagged describes_superseded_world, with no checkpoint artifact and no artifact of an earlier head of this branch. Confirm the report states the provenance distinction explicitly: the candidate's internal/app/app.go predates the landed #22093 signing changes because the branch is based on 359b211c, the clean merge tree onto c0108be8 carries those changes forward, and the absence of the superseded-world flag is not evidence that they are in the candidate snapshot. State the three conclusions explicitly. Architecture: name the affected layers, say whether the head preserves or changes their contract, confirm the same-head pages and both notes agree with the code and with each other, and confirm the head neither delivers nor claims any outcome of stages 0e7c04c8 (#22311) or 8415ce89 (#22312) and does not close or narrow parent #22168. Security: confirm the conservative direction holds everywhere -- no response advertises delete advice while its bounded observation is incomplete, unknown still protects, no missing answer became a negative, and the earlier trust boundary is intact. Simplification: identify any opportunity to simplify without weakening these conditions. File the verdict with gs review naming the internal/app/association.go artifact first. Attached: gates-s1-v6.txt, eight gates all rc=0 and all fresh at this head with the docset gate green on its first run; and mutants-s1.txt, twenty-seven omission controls run against this tree, twenty-five re-run with m23 retargeted plus m26-predicate-ignores-table and m27-withheld-reason-generic, none surviving.","head":"a5c0889fa3e08f048eb1b09057af26da62ad3882","no_git_artifact":"true","stale_bases":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:5343a64b18dc8308a42e692e7bb9383886677005, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ac54fe712abc7ca0614ca1067bad1a7ff8c490b0, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:e389d8562ce26bac1f65c07f251d7260d4e21fd4 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0011eb5371d0d5e24d03050e4dc9ed9219c4a09c, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:00ab40412a425f9430f6a8b56519e53b390991e7, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:0169ac61d80658478db25ce500d46d087c06fdde, git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:01c4e8b02601b3b35241c2baffd15e16e3e3d09d and 168 more","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"